## LOAD (binding; the MPS heavy-slot convention, as B692)
8 cores. Heavy = `dotnet restore/build/test`, `npm ci`, `eslint`, `playwright --list`, `tsc`. A git command, a `gh` GET, a Jira GET, a python program is light. **Start a heavy run only while `uptime` load1 is below 7** at two reads 4 s apart (not independent; say so), recorded beside the result; at 7 or over poll every 20 s, log each wait, do the light work meanwhile (C1 programs, C3 hygiene and polling, C4). Then take ONE slot atomically: `mkdir '<project>/.tools/heavy-slot-1' 2>/dev/null || mkdir '<project>/.tools/heavy-slot-2' 2>/dev/null || mkdir '<project>/.tools/heavy-slot-3'`, write `B696 <time> <label>` into its `holder`; read every other holder first and NEVER touch another seat's holder (the live MPS seats M26 and G125 hold slots at times; a holder that looks stale is reported, not moved; all three held: wait 3 minutes and retry). At most ONE slot and one heavy job at a time; re-read load1 at the start of each job; a seat-local `.tools/b696/heavy.lock` (directory, pid inside, released by MOVE) keeps your own waiters to one job; release only your own slot, by a MOVE into `.tools/_quarantine/2026-10-25_B696_<slot>_<label>/`, never `rm`. The shared `.tools/.git-lock` wraps git ref writes only. A MOVING heartbeat (a counter plus `date`) goes to `.tools/b696/logs/heartbeat.txt` every 30 s from every waiter; a figure that does not move for 3 beats means stuck: say so. A wait loop never uses `pgrep -f` or `ps | grep` on its own pattern: wait on a recorded PID with `kill -0` or a done-marker file. Every command gets its input or none (`/dev/null`). No artificial load. NuGet and npm homes are SEAT-LOCAL, set before the first restore or install.

